## QUESTION : LOAD BALANCER TRAFFIC KO SERVERS KE BEECH DISTRIBUTE KAISE KARTA HAI ?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Traffic ko backend servers ke beech distribute karne ke liye Load Balancer different algorithms use karta hai,
jaise:

1: ROUND ROBIN
-----------------

- Requests ko servers ke beech ek-ek karke, cyclic order me distribute karta hai.

 Example:

 Suppose 3 servers hain:

 Server A
 Server B
 Server C

 Requests aayi: R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6

 Distribution:

 R1 → Server A
 R2 → Server B
 R3 → Server C
 R4 → Server A (wapas se shuru)
 R5 → Server B
 R6 → Server C


### QUESTION : KAB USE KAREIN ?
---------------------------------

- Sab servers ki capacity barabar ho
- Requests roughly similar ho (koi heavy, koi light nahi)



2: WEIGHTED ROUND ROBIN
---------------------------

- Round Robin ka advanced version.
- Jab servers ki capacity alag-alag ho, to har server ko uski capacity ke according requests distribute ki jaati hain.
- Zyada powerful server = zyada requests.


Example:

Suppose 3 servers hain, lekin capacity alag-alag hai:

    Server A → 16-core CPU  (very powerful)  → Weight = 4
    Server B → 8-core CPU   (medium)         → Weight = 2
    Server C → 4-core CPU   (weak)           → Weight = 1

Total Weight = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7

Ab 7 requests aayi: R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7

Distribution (weight ke according):

    R1 → Server A
    R2 → Server A
    R3 → Server A
    R4 → Server A   (Server A ko 4 requests — weight 4)
    R5 → Server B
    R6 → Server B   (Server B ko 2 requests — weight 2)
    R7 → Server C   (Server C ko 1 request — weight 1)


### QUESTION : KAB USE KAREIN ?
---------------------------------
- Servers ki hardware capacity alag ho
- Kuch servers ko intentionally zyada traffic dena ho (canary deployment, A/B testing)



3: LEAST CONNECTIONS
------------------------

Request us server ko bheji jaati hai jiske paas sabse kam active connections ho.
Ye real-time load dekh kar decide karta hai.

Example:

Current state:

Server A → 50 active connections
Server B → 10 active connections
Server C → 30 active connections

Nayi request → Server B (sabse kam)


### QUESTION : KAB USE KAREIN ?
---------------------------------

- Servers ki hardware capacity alag ho
- Requests ki processing time alag-alag ho (kuch 10ms, kuch 5 seconds)



4: IP HASH (STICKY SESSIONS)
------------------------------

- Is algorithm me user ke IP address ka hash calculate karke decide kiya jaata hai ki 
  us user ka traffic kis particular server par bhejna hai.
- Matlab: Same client → hamesha same server
- Formula: hash(user_ip) % number_of_servers = Server

 Example:

 Suppose 3 servers hain:

 Server A
 Server B
 Server C

 Ek user aata hai:

 User IP = 49.205.10.20

 Load Balancer hash calculate karta hai:

 hash(49.205.10.20) = 12345
 12345 % 3 = 0 → Server A

 Ab ye user jitni bhi requests bhejega, sab Server A par hi jaayengi.


### QUESTION : KAB USE KAREIN ?
----------------------------------

Jab Sticky Sessions chahiye — jaise distributed environment me 
application HttpSession use karti ho authentication/authorization 
ke liye.

Sticky Session me LB user ko ek particular server se bind kar 
deta hai, aur us user ki saari subsequent requests usi bound 
server par bhejta hai.

Isi binding ko achieve karne ke liye LB me IP Hash algorithm 
use kiya jaata hai.



### QUESTION: IP HASH KA PROBLEM KYA HAI ?
-------------------------------------------

IP Hash ka formula tha:

    hash(user_ip) % number_of_servers = Server

Problem: Jab bhi servers ki count change hoti hai (server add ya 
remove), to `% number_of_servers` ka value bhi change ho jaata hai.

Result: Lagbhag saare users ka server mapping badal jaata hai.

Agar ye LB Sticky Sessions maintain karne ke liye use ho raha
tha (i.e. user ka HTTP Session ek particular server par stored
tha), to mapping change hone ki wajah se user ki subsequent
request ab ek naye server par route ho jaayegi.

Naye server ke paas us user ka HTTP Session available nahi hoga,
jiski wajah se session lost ho jaayega — user ko dobara login
karna pad sakta hai ya request fail ho sakti hai.



5: CONSISTENT HASHING 
------------------------

Consistent Hashing bhi IP Hash ki tarah "Same client → Same server" 
mapping maintain karta hai. Lekin IP Hash ke comparison me servers 
add/remove hone par ye comparatively kam users ka server 
mapping change karta hai.

Agar N servers hain, to server add/remove hone par sirf ~1/N users 
ka mapping change hota hai. Baaki (N-1)/N users apne existing server 
par hi route hote rehte hain.Isliye total users ka sirf ~1/N portion
ki user-server mapping ko migrate karna padta hai,  baaki 
(N-1)/N users apne existing server par hi route hote rehte hain.

Iske ulta IP Hash me (hash(client_ip) % number_of_servers) — 
number_of_servers change hote hi almost saare users ka server
mapping badal jaata hai (rehashing problem).


## QUESTION: HASH SPACE AUR HASH RING KYA HAI?
--------------------------------------------------

Hash Function kisi bhi input ko ek fixed numeric range ke andar 
convert karta hai.

    hash(input) → 0 to 359 (ya koi bhi fixed range)

Is fixed numeric range ko Hash Space kehte hain.

Jab is Hash Space ko circular form me represent kiya jaata hai, 
to use Hash Ring kehte hain.

    Hash Space   →  Linear range (0 to 359)
    Hash Ring    →  Same range, circular form me



## CONSISTENT HASHING KAISE SOLVE KARTA HAI?
----------------------------------------------

Consistent Hashing me `% number_of_servers` wala formula use nahi 
hota. Iske bajay ek Hash Ring use hota hai.

Concept:

- Server ka koi unique identifier hash function me pass kiya jaata hai.
  Ye identifier kuch bhi ho sakta hai — jaise Server ka hostname
  ya IP address. Us identifier ki hash value ke basis par server ko
  hash ring par place kiya jaata hai.

- User ka koi unique identifier hash function me pass kiya jaata hai.
  Ye identifier kuch bhi ho sakta hai — jaise User ka IP address
  ya  User ID. Us identifier ki hash value ke basis par user ko
  hash ring par place kiya jaata hai. 

- User ki hashe value se clockwise direction me jo pehla 
  Server Node milega, us user ke traffic us server par route 
  kiya jaata hai.



## QUESTION : HASH INPUT KE LIYE KIS IDENTIFIER KO USE KARNA CHAHIYE — USER KE LIYE AUR SERVER KE LIYE ?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Hash input ke liye IP address use karna ideal nahi hota — na user ke liye, 
na server ke liye.

### USER IDENTIFIER
--------------------

IP address ko hash input banana problematic hai:

- Mobile users ka IP change hota rehta hai (network switch)
- Office NAT ke peeche saare users same public IP share karte hain → Same hash → Sab ek hi server par chale jaayenge

Isi wajah se user-server mapping baar-baar badalta rehta hai.

Isliye production me usually User ID, Session Cookie, ya JWT ka 
sub claim use kiya jaata hai as hash input.


### SERVER IDENTIFIER
-----------------------

Cloud me server ka IP change hota rehta hai (auto-scaling, container restart).
IP ko hash input banaya to server ring par apni position badal dega,
aur uske saare users ka mapping bhi badal jaayega.

Isliye stable unique ID prefer karte hain — Node ID, hostname, ya Instance ID.


## QUESTION : CONSISTENT HASHING KI LIMITATION ? - UNEVEN SERVER DISTRIBUTION ON THE RING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Server ka identifier hash karke ring par place kiya jaata hai. Ye hash value random hoti hai,
isliye servers ring par hamesha evenly distribute nahi hote.

Kabhi-kabhi 2-3 servers ring par ek hi jagah paas-paas cluster ho jaate hain,
jabki baaki ring ka bada portion khaali rehta hai.


### QUESTION : HAR SERVER PAR KITNA LOAD AAYEGA YE KAISE DECIDE HOTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ring par har server apne preceding arc ka traffic handle karta hai.

Preceding arc matlab: us server se pehle wale server se lekar apne tak ka clockwise distance.

Jitna bada arc → Utna zyada load.
Jitna chhota arc → Utna kam load.

Example:

Manlo hash function ka range hai 0 to 360 (yani ring ka total size 360 units hai)

Servers ki positions ring par:

Server A → Position 100
Server B → Position 105
Server C → Position 110
Server D → Position 300


Ring par aise dikhega:


                        0 / 360
                           |
                           |
                           |
           Server D        |        Server A
           (300)           |        (100)
