## QUESTION : LOAD BALANCER KE TYPES — OSI LAYER KE BASIS PAR ?
-----------------------------------------------------------------

OSI layer ke basis par Load Balancers mainly 2 types ke hote hain:

1. L4 Load Balancer (Transport Layer)
2. L7 Load Balancer (Application Layer)



1: L4 LOAD BALANCER (TRANSPORT LAYER)
---------------------------------------

L4 LB OSI model ke Transport Layer (Layer 4) par kaam karta hai.

Ye sirf IP Address aur Port Number dekh sakta hai.


### QUESTION : KYA L4 LB NAHI DEKH SAKTA ?
---------------------------------------------

- URL kya hai? (/api/users ya /api/orders)
- HTTP method kya hai? (GET, POST, DELETE)
- Headers me kya hai? (JWT token, cookies)
- Body me kya hai?


### QUESTION : KYA L4 LB KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI ?
-------------------------------------------------

Suppose tumhare paas 3 servers hain:

    Server A → 10.0.0.1:8080
    Server B → 10.0.0.2:8080
    Server C → 10.0.0.3:8080

LB ka public IP: 1.2.3.4
Port → 443    (HTTPS traffic ke liye)


### FINAL FLOW :
-----------------

1 - Client and LB ke beech TCP 3 way handshake hota hai, ye sucessfull hone ke
    baad Client aur LB ke beech TCP connection establish ho jata hai.

2 - Client apna TLS ClientHello bhejta hai (ye bytes hain — TLS handshake ka pehla message).
    LB inhe padh NAHI sakta — usse sirf Source/Destination IP aur Port dikhta hai.

3 - LB apne algorithm (Round Robin / Least Conn / etc.) ke basis par
    decide karta hai — "Ye request Server B ko bhej dete hain."

4-  LB aur Server B ke beech TCP 3 way handshake hota hai, ye successful hone ke 
    baad LB aur Server B ke beech ek naya TCP connection establish ho jaata hai.

5-  LB wahi encrypted TLS ClientHello bytes Server B ko forward
    kar deta hai (SSL Passthrough — bina decrypt kiye).

6-  Server B apna TLS ServerHello (ClientHello ka response)
    aur Certificate bhejta hai. Ye TLS handshake messages hain
    TLS 1.2 me plaintext hote hain. Lekin L4 LB inhe inspect
    nahi karta — bas bytes forward kar deta hai.

    ⚠️ IMPORTANT:

    - TLS 1.2 me ServerHello + Certificate dono plaintext hote hain.
    - TLS 1.3 me sirf ServerHello plaintext hota hai, Certificate encrypted hota hai.
    - Lekin L4 LB ke liye ye baat matter nahi karti — wo bas bytes forward karta hai.

7- Ab TLS session established ho gaya — Client aur Server B ke beech (end-to-end).

    ⚠️ IMPORTANT:
        - LB is TLS handshake me participate NAHI karta.
        - LB bas encrypted bytes idhar se udhar forward kar raha tha.

8-  Client encrypted HTTPS request bhejta hai (HTTPS = HTTP + TLS; HTTP request TLS ke through encrypted hai) LB ko
    LB wahi encrypted bytes Server B ko forward kar deta hai.
    Server B decrypt karta hai, process karta hai.

9-  Server B encrypted HTTPS response bhejta hai.
    LB wahi bytes Client ko forward kar deta hai.
    Client decrypt karta hai.


### IMPORTANT POINT :
-----------------------

1. Client ↔ LB    →  Sirf TCP handshake
2. LB ↔ Server B  →  Sirf TCP handshake
3. Client ↔ Server B  →  TLS handshake (end-to-end)
4. HTTPS request →  Client Server B ko bhejta hai (encrypted),
                    LB sirf forward karta hai

LB ek dumb pipe ki tarah kaam karta hai — bytes ko aage bhejta hai,
par unhe samajhta nahi, decrypt nahi karta.Isi ko SSL Passthrough kehte hain.



2: L7 LOAD BALANCER (Application Layer)
-----------------------------------------

L7 LB OSI model ke Application Layer (Layer 7) par kaam karta hai.


### QUESTION : L7 LB KYA DEKH SAKTA HAI ?
--------------------------------------------

L7 LB poori HTTP/HTTPS request padh sakta hai. Usse ye sab dikhta hai:

- IP Address       (Source IP, Destination IP)
- Port Number      (Source Port, Destination Port)
- URL kya hai?     (/api/users ya /api/orders)
- HTTP method?     (GET, POST, DELETE)
- Headers me kya?  (JWT token, cookies, content-type)
- Body me kya hai?
- Host kya hai?    (myapp.com, admin.myapp.com)
- Cookies          (JSESSIONID, AWSALB, etc.)


⚠️ IMPORTANT:

OSI layers nested hoti hain — Layer 7 tak pahunchne ke liye
data Layer 4 se hoke guzarta hai. Isliye L7 LB ko IP aur Port ki
information bhi already available hoti hai.



### QUESTION : L7 LB KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------

Suppose tumhare paas ek **User Service** hai, aur uske multiple
instances different servers par run kar rahe hain:

    Server A → User Service Instance 1 → 10.0.0.1:8080
    Server B → User Service Instance 2 → 10.0.0.2:8080
    Server C → User Service Instance 3 → 10.0.0.3:8080


LB ka public IP: 1.2.3.4
Port → 443    (HTTPS traffic ke liye)


### FINAL FLOW :
-----------------

1 - Client aur LB ke beech TCP 3-way handshake hota hai. Successful
    hone ke baad Client aur LB ke beech TCP connection establish
    ho jaata hai.

2 - Ab Client TLS handshake initiate karta hai LB ke saath.
    Iske liye Client sabse pehla TLS handshake message — TLS ClientHello —
    LB ko bhejta hai, taaki HTTPS connection establish kiya ja sake.

3 - LB ClientHello ka response me apna TLS ServerHello bhejta hai,
    aur apna SSL certificate bhi Client ko present karta hai.
    Client certificate verify karta hai, aur dono sides encryption
    algorithm aur session keys par agree karte hain.
    TLS handshake successful hone par Client aur LB ke beech ek
    encrypted channel establish ho jaata hai.

4 - Ab Client HTTPS request bhejta hai LB ko.
    Ye request encrypted bytes ke form me LB tak pahunchti hai.
    LB un encrypted bytes ko DECRYPT karta hai.

    Isi ko SSL Termination kehte hain.

    Yaani HTTPS connection LB par terminate ho jaata hai.

5 - Decrypt hone ke baad LB ke paas plain HTTP request hoti hai:

        Method   → GET
        URL      → /api/users/123
        Host     → myapp.com
        Headers  → Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
        Body     → (empty in this case)

    Ab LB request ko padh sakta hai. 


6- LB apne algorithm (Round Robin / Least Conn / etc.) ke basis par
   decide karta hai — "Ye request Server B ko bhej dete hain."


7 - Ab LB aur Server B ke beech TCP 3-way handshake hota hai.
    Successful hone ke baad LB aur Server B ke beech TCP connection
    establish ho jaata hai.


8- LB request ko Server B ko forward karta hai. Yahan do possibilities hain:

     Option 1:  LB → Server B (HTTP)     ← plain HTTP (jo Client se aayi thi, SSL termination ke baad decrypt hui thi)
     Option 2:  LB → Server B (HTTPS)    ← naya TLS session

     Ye architecture par depend karta hai. Agar backend services internal
     network me hain (secure VLAN me), to HTTP bhi kaafi hota hai. Agar
     stricter security chahiye, to LB backend ke saath naya TLS session
     establish karta hai.

     Agar TLS session chahiye, to Client aur LB wale jaisa hi TLS handshake
     hoga. Successful TLS handshake ke baad LB Server B ko HTTPS request
     bhej sakta hai.


9 - Server B(Instance) request process karta hai aur response generate karta hai.

    Yahan bhi do possibilities hain — depending on Step 8 me kaunsa
    option use hua tha:

    Option 1 (LB → Server B HTTP tha):
        - Server B plain HTTP response bhejta hai LB ko.
        - Ab LB ke paas plaintext response aa jaata hai.

    Option 2 (LB → Server B HTTPS tha):
        - Server B apne TLS Session keys (jo LB ↔ Server B ke beech wale TLS session ki keys hain)
          se response encrypt karta hai.
        - Encrypted bytes LB ko bhejta hai.
        - LB un bytes ko DECRYPT karta hai using the same TLS Session keys (LB ↔ Server B wale session ki keys).
        - LB ke paas ab plaintext HTTP response aa jaata hai.


10 - Ab LB ke paas plaintext HTTP response hai (kisi bhi option me).

     Ab LB is response ko Client tak pahunchane ke liye TLS Session ki keys (jo LB ↔ Client ke beech wale TLS session ki keys hai) 
     se ENCRYPT karta hai and  Encrypted bytes Client ko bhejta hai.

11 - Client encrypted bytes receive karta hai.
     Client same TLS Session ki keys (LB ↔  Client wale session ki keys) se DECRYPT karta hai.
     Client ko plaintext HTTP response mil jaata hai ✅




### QUESTION : L7 LOAD BALANCER KI EXTRA CAPABILITIES KYA HAIN ?
-----------------------------------------------------------------

1: HEALTH CHECKS
------------------

Ye L4/L7 LB dono karte hain (common capability).

LB do tarike se detect karta hai ki server healthy hai ya nahi.


#### Active Health Check
-------------------------

LB khud periodically server ke /health endpoint par request bhejta hai.
Agar response nahi aaya ya error aaya, to server unhealthy mark karta hai.


#### Passive Health Check
---------------------------

LB live traffic observe karta hai. Agar koi server baar-baar
failures/timeouts de raha hai, to usse unhealthy mark karta hai.


#### NOTE:
-----------

LB turant unhealthy mark nahi karta. Configured consecutive failures
(e.g. 3) ke baad hi unhealthy maanta hai — taaki temporary glitch
ki wajah se server pool se na hat jaaye.


#### FINAL BEHAVIOUR:
-----------------------

Dono cases me LB us unhealthy server ka traffic baaki healthy servers
me distribute kar deta hai.


#### PRODUCTION ME:
--------------------

Real systems me Active aur Passive — dono saath use hote hain.

Active jaldi detect karta hai ki server down hai. Passive actual
failure pattern pakadta hai — jaise server HTTP 200 de raha hai
lekin slow hai. Dono combine karke LB sahi decision leta hai.




2: PATH BASED ROUTING
-----------------------

    /api/users/**    → User Service
    /api/orders/**   → Order Service
    /api/payments/** → Payment Service

Ye ek prominent feature hai jo actually **API Gateway** ka hota hai —
alag-alag services ke beech URL ke basis par routing karna.

Lekin L7 LB bhi ye kaam kar sakta hai, kyunki wo request ka URL
padh sakta hai (jo L4 LB nahi kar sakta).

Isi wajah se L7 LB aur API Gateway ka overlap bahut zyada hai.
Kai architectures me dono ka kaam ek hi component kar deta hai —
jaise AWS ALB ya NGINX (Proxy).




3: RATE LIMITING
------------------

Rate Limiting ka matlab: Ek client/user kitni requests bhej sakta hai,
uski limit set karna.


A:  IP-BASED RATE LIMITING 
-----------------------------

Example : /login → 5 requests/min per IP

Ek IP address se 1 minute me maximum 5 baar hi login try kar sakte ho.

Real-world: Bank login page, OTP verification, ATM PIN — sabme
rate limiting hoti hai.


#### QUESTION : IP-BASED RATE LIMITING KAB USE KARTE HAIN ?
-------------------------------------------------------------

IP-based rate limiting un endpoints ke liye use karte hain jahan
user ka authenticated identity available nahi hota — jaise Login,
Register, Forgot Password, OTP Send, ya public/anonymous APIs.

NAT issue yahan bhi hota hai — kyunki dher saare users ka NAT/PAT
translation ke baad sabka same public IP ho jaata hai (office NAT
ke peeche 100 users, sab ek hi public IP share karte hain).

Isliye limit thodi loose rakhte hain (jaise 5/min per IP) taaki
genuine users impact na hon.


##### QUESTION : AGAR ATTACKER DIFFERENT IPs SE DHER SAARI LOGIN REQUESTS BHEJTA HAI TO?
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sirf IP-based limit kaafi nahi hoti — kyunki har request alag IP
se aa rahi hai, aur per-IP limit easily bypass ho jaayegi.


1: Login Endpoints 
-------------------

   Login/Register/OTP jaise endpoints me humare paas user ka
   claimed identifier bhi hota hai — Email, Mobile Number, Username.
   (Claimed isliye, kyunki user ne bas khud bata diya hai ki wo kaun hai. Verify nahi hua hai.)

   To hum identifier-based rate limiting lagate hain:

    - 5 requests/min per Email
    - 3 requests/10min per Mobile

   Ab chahe attacker 100 different IPs use kare, agar wo same
   email/mobile par attack kar raha hai, to identifier-based limit
   usse rok legi — jaise 10 failed login attempts → Account
   lockout 30 mins.


2: Truly Anonymous Endpoints (Public APIs)
---------------------------------------------

Agar endpoint sach me anonymous hai — jaise Weather API, Exchange
Rate API, Stock Price API — yahan user ka koi identifier hi nahi
hota. Sirf IP bacha hai.

Is case me:

1. Per IP limit (medium, loose) — jaise 1000 req/hour per IP
2. Agar API keys issue ki hain, to per API key limit — ye
   IP-independent hoti hai, isliye NAT problem bhi solve ho jaata hai

Aur agar koi API key bhi nahi hai (bilkul open API), to NAT
problem ka koi perfect solution nahi hota. Isliye limit loose
rakhni padti hai.




2: USER-BASED RATE LIMITING 
---------------------------------

Example : /api/** → 100 requests/min per user

Ek logged-in user 1 minute me maximum 100 API calls kar sakta hai.


Yahan "per IP" nahi, "per user" hai, Kyunki ek IP ke peeche 100 users ho 
sakte hain (office NAT). Har user ka private IP NAT/PAT ke through ek public
IP me convert hota hai, aur wahi public IP internet par dikhta hai — jis wajah
se dher saare users ke liye same public IP dikh sakta hai.

Agar hum IP ke basis par rate limiting lagayenge, toh ek user ke limit exceed karne 
par usi public IP ke peeche maujood baaki users bhi block ho sakte hain.

Isliye authenticated requests ke liye generally per-user rate limiting use karte hain.

User ko uniquely identify karne ke liye hum JWT token se sub claim (user ID)
extract karte hain. Ye user ki stable identity hai — same user ka same sub
hota hai, chahe kitne bhi naye tokens issue hon.

Isse har user ki API request count independently track hoti hai aur
rate limit bhi individually enforce hoti hai.



3: MOBILE NUMBER BASED RATE LIMITING 
---------------------------------------

Example : /otp/send → 3 requests/10min per mobile number

Ek mobile number par 10 minute me maximum 3 hi OTP bhej sakte ho.

Kyun?

Problem: OTP SMS bhejne ka cost hota hai (SMS gateway ka paisa lagta hai).
Agar koi attacker script chala kar 10,000 OTP bhejwa de kisi number pe:

    - Company ka paisa waste hoga (10,000 SMS ka bill)
    - Victim user ko spam milta rahega — uske phone me OTP messages
      ki barsaat

Ye SMS bombing attack kehlata hai.

Real-world: IRCTC, PhonePe, Paytm, WhatsApp — sabme OTP rate limiting
hoti hai.




4: REQUEST TRANSFORMATION
----------------------------

L7 LB client request ko modify kar sakta hai before forwarding to
the backend services. Isliye backend services ko ye kaam nahi
karna padta.


Common examples:


1. HEADER ADD KARNA
---------------------

   a) X-FORWARDED-FOR HEADER ADD KARNA
   -------------------------------------

   LB original client IP ko `X-Forwarded-For` header me add karta
   hai before forwarding to backend.

   Backend ko by default LB ka IP dikhta hai, actual client ka nahi.
   Isliye LB original client IP ko header me add kar deta hai:

   X-Forwarded-For: 49.205.10.20

   
   Kyun zaroori hai:
       - Backend me per-client-IP rate limiting lagane ke liye
       - Audit logs me actual client IP record karne ke liye

   Fintech/bank me ye compliance requirement hai (PCI DSS, RBI)
      — audit trail me client IP hona mandatory hota hai.


   b) X-Request-ID — Unique Request Identifier
   --------------------------------------------
    LB har request me `X-Request-ID: uuid` add kar deta hai.

    Isse distributed tracing me help milti hai — ek hi request ko
    multiple services ke across uniquely identify kiya ja sakta hai.
    Agar koi issue aaye, to us request ID se saari service logs
    me filter karke trace kar sakte hain.


2. HEADER REMOVE KARNA
------------------------
   Client se aaye internal headers strip kar deta hai (jaise
   `X-Internal-Debug`), taaki backend pe unintended effect na ho.


3. PATH PREFIX STRIP
----------------------
   Client bhejta hai:  /api/users/123
   LB backend ko bhejta hai:  /users/123

   Ye common hai microservices me — backend ko `/api` prefix
   ke baare me pata hi nahi hota.




5: RESPONSE TRANSFORMATION
----------------------------

L7 LB Backend response ko modify kar sakta hai before forwarding to
the client.

Common examples:

1. HEADER ADD KARNA
----------------------
   Security headers add karna — jaise `X-Frame-Options`,
   `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Content-Type-Options`.

2. HEADER REMOVE KARNA
-----------------------
   Backend ke internal headers strip karna — jaise server
   version, internal service names. Security ke liye important.

3. RESPONSE BODY MODIFY KARNA
-------------------------------
   Kam common, lekin possible — sensitive fields hide karna
   (salary, internal IDs) before sending to client.



6: COMPRESSION
----------------

LB Backend ke response ko compress karke Client ko bhejta hai.

Primary purpose:

    - Network bandwidth reduce karna, jisse network transfer
      time kam hota hai aur overall network performance
      improve hota hai.


#### KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI?
----------------------------

Client request me `Accept-Encoding: gzip, br` header bhejta hai —
matlab "main gzip aur Brotli compression ko samajh sakta hoon."

LB ye header dekhta hai, aur saath hi response ka Content-Type bhi
check karta hai. Agar client ne compression support kiya hai AND
content compressible hai (jaise plain JSON), to LB usse compress
karke Client ko bhejta hai.

    Client ←(gzip)←  LB  ←(plain JSON)←  Backend


Client ka browser automatically decompress kar leta hai —
developer ko kuch nahi karna padta.

Slow networks (mobile) me bahut bada difference padta hai
compression ka.


#### NOTE:
----------
Compression ke liye do cheezein chahiye — client support kare
(Accept-Encoding) AND content compressible ho (text-based).
Agar client ne gzip maang liya bhi, lekin response JPEG/MP4 hai,
to LB compress nahi karega kyunki already compressed content
ko dobara compress karne se fayda nahi hota, sirf CPU waste hota hai.


#### COMMON ALGORITHMS:
-----------------------

- GZIP → Most common, universally supported
- Brotli (br) → Modern, better compression ratio



7: CACHING
------------

LB frequently requested responses ko apne paas cache kar leta hai.
Baar-baar backend tak request nahi jaati.

#### QUESTION : KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI?
-----------------------------------------

1. Client request bhejta hai: GET /product/101
2. LB check karta hai — "Ye response mere cache me hai?"
3. Agar hai (CACHE HIT): LB directly Client ko return kar deta hai, backend ko request hi nahi jaati.
4. Agar nahi (CACHE MISS): LB backend se fetch karta hai, response cache me store karta hai (agar cacheable hai to), aur Client ko return karta hai.


#### QUESTION : LB KAISE DECIDE KARTA HAI KI RESPONSE KO CACHE KARNA HAI KI NAHI ?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LB cache karne ka decision khud nahi leta. Backend response ke saath Cache-Control header bhejta hai — jaise public, max-age=60. 
LB us header ko respect karta hai aur us duration tak response cache karta hai. Agar no-store hai to cache nahi karega. 
Ye decision backend engineer ka hota hai, kyunki usse best pata hota hai ki kaunsa data cacheable hai aur kaunsa nahi.

#### QUESTION :  KYUN USEFUL HAI?
----------------------------------

- Backend load kam hota hai
- Response time bahut fast ho jaata hai (LB ke paas hi data hai)
- Network bandwidth bachta hai

#### IMPORTANT:
---------------

- Sirf GET / HEAD requests cache hoti hain (safe, read-only methods).

- POST / PUT / DELETE / PATCH technically cache ho sakti hain
  (POST spec ke according), lekin practically nahi karte — kyunki
  ye state change karte hain, jisse inconsistency create hoti hai.



8: SSL/TLS TERMINATION
------------------------

- Client aur LB ke beech pehle TCP 3-way handshake hota hai → TCP connection establish.
- Uske baad Client aur LB ke beech TLS handshake hota hai → encrypted channel establish.
- TLS Handshake complete hone ke baad Client aur Server ke beech
  ek secure HTTPS connection establish ho jata hai.
- Ab Client HTTPS request (HTTP + TLS) bhejta hai LB ko — encrypted form me.
- LB us encrypted HTTP request ko decrypt karta hai.
- Yahi point hai jahan Client aur LB ke beech TLS connection terminate ho jaata hai.
  Isi ko SSL/TLS Termination kehte hain.

Matlab: Layer 7 LB SSL/TLS Termination capable hai. L4 LB nahi — wo sirf SSL/TLS Passthrough 
karta hai (encrypted bytes waise hi forward).


### NOTE :
-----------

- SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) aur TLS (Transport Layer Security) cryptographic protocols hain, jo browser aur server ke beech data ko encrypt karke secure karte hain.
- TLS, SSL ka modern aur secure version hai. SSL security vulnerabilities ki wajah se deprecated ho chuka hai. Aaj bhi commonly TLS ko “SSL” kaha jata hai.



### QUESTION : SSL/TLS TERMINATION KE BENEFITS KYA HAIN ?
-------------------------------------------------------------

1. Backend services ko SSL/TLS handshake nahi karna padta
------------------------------------------------------------

   LB khud Client ke saath TLS handshake karta hai. Uske baad
   backend services ko plain HTTP request forward karta hai.
   Backend services ko TLS handshake, certificate verify,
   encryption/decryption — kuch nahi karna padta.


2. Centralized Certificate Management
---------------------------------------

   SSL certificate sirf LB par rakhna padta hai. Agar 100 backend
   services hain, to sabke liye alag-alag certificate manage nahi
   karna padta. Ek jagah certificate renew karo — sab services
   automatically updated ho jaati hain.



### NOTE : YE ARCHITECTURE DEPENDENT HAI
--------------------------------------------

LB backend ko plain HTTP bhejega ya HTTPS — ye architecture par
depend karta hai.

Agar backend services LB ke secure internal network me hain
(trusted VLAN), to plain HTTP theek hai.

Lekin agar stricter security chahiye (zero-trust, sensitive data),
to LB backend ke saath naya TLS session establish karta hai aur
HTTPS request bhejta hai. Is case me Client-LB aur LB-Backend
do independent TLS sessions hote hain.




